
Please read the paper at http://www.staikos.net/~staikos/papers/2003/
It has been updated to 10/22/2003.

-----------

This directory consists of two libraries: kwalletbackend and kwalletclient

KWallet::Backend is used inside kded to manage the actual files and
encryption.  On the other hand, KWallet::Wallet is used to represent
wallet entries and to encapsulate communication to and from the
wallet server inside kded.


Application level:

KSystemTray notifier for wallet accesses

Konqueror plugin

KControl module



The Secret Service backend (kio/misc/kwalletd/kwalletsecretservice.*):

With libsecret (KDE3_WITH_LIBSECRET), kwalletd can keep the wallets in
the freedesktop.org Secret Service (org.freedesktop.secrets: the GNOME
keyring, KeePassXC, ...) instead of the encrypted files.  The GNOME login
keyring is unlocked by the display manager (pam_gnome_keyring), so the
wallet opens without a password, as in GNOME.  kwalletrc:

  [Wallet]
  Backend=SecretService   always (opening fails if the service is gone)
  Backend=File            the classic encrypted files
  (unset)                 the Secret Service if org.freedesktop.secrets
                          is on the session bus when kwalletd starts,
                          else the files
  Secret Service Prompt on Open=false
                          the "allow this application" dialog for the
                          Secret Service (off by default, like GNOME)

Every entry is an item of the default collection ("login"), schema
"org.kde.kwallet3", attributes wallet, folder, key and type
(password | map | stream | unknown | binary | folder), label
"KWallet: <folder>/<key>".  Passwords are UTF-8 text/plain; maps keep
KWallet's QDataStream encoding (application/x-kde-kwallet-map); streams
are the bytes (application/octet-stream); a folder (also an empty one)
has a marker item with type "folder" and key "".  kwalletd only ever
touches items of this schema.  A locked collection is unlocked through
the service's own prompt; there is no KDE password dialog.  There is no
wallet password to change (changePassword() says so); Seahorse shows and
edits the items too.

Migration: when a wallet is opened in the Secret Service and a classic
file of the same name exists (share/apps/kwallet/<wallet>.kwl), kwalletd
offers once to import it (this needs the old password once).  Entries
that already exist in the Secret Service are kept; when everything was
copied the file is renamed to <wallet>.kwl.migrated (never deleted).
"Do Not Import" is remembered in kwalletrc [Secret Service Import]; the
import can be started later with
  dcop kded kwalletd importClassicWallet <wallet> 0
or with KWalletManager's Import Wallet.

Tests: cmake -DKDE3_BUILD_KWALLETD_TESTS=ON, then
  kio/misc/kwalletd/tests/run-tests.sh <build dir> [xdrv]
(a private D-Bus session and gnome-keyring, never the user's).
